// v0.2 original editorial expansions, same stable IDs
const EDITORIAL=[["Cue → response → reward","Imagine reading two pages while your coffee brews. Coffee is already part of the day, so the book has a natural place in it. Compare that with hiding the book in a drawer and waiting for motivation. A reward need not be a badge: an interesting idea or a satisfying story may be enough. Notice which reading experiences you actually want to repeat, rather than assuming a streak will motivate you. The objective is to build an association between a familiar situation and an activity you value."],["Your actions | External outcomes","Before a difficult conversation, you can prepare your evidence, choose a respectful tone and listen carefully. You cannot decide how the other person responds. This distinction does not prohibit trying to influence an outcome; it helps you judge your own effort without pretending to control the world. Write two columns before an important task: choices that are yours and circumstances you can only respond to. Put your next action in the first column."],["Impulse → chosen response","A public criticism can provoke a harsh reply that feels powerful for a moment but leaves a conflict to repair. Restraint is different from refusing to address a genuine wrong. You can defend a principle firmly and still govern your tone. Proverbs uses contrasting images to challenge familiar notions of power. Read the saying alongside its surrounding teachings about speech, pride and judgment rather than treating it as an isolated motivational slogan."],["Sunlight → viewing angle","Hold a ball near a lamp. One side receives light while the other remains dark. Walk around it and the illuminated portion appears to change even though the lamp lights the same half. Around new moon, the illuminated side mostly faces away from us; around full moon, it mostly faces toward us. Eclipses involve special alignments and do not explain ordinary lunar phases. The model makes the monthly cycle something you can reason about instead of merely memorize."],["Read → retrieve → check","After a page, put the book aside and explain the main idea in your own words. You may remember a phrase yet be unable to explain the author's reasoning. That gap is useful feedback. Return to the text and repair the missing connection, then try again later. Familiarity can feel deceptively similar to understanding when the source remains open. Recall does not replace careful reading; it makes your learning more visible. Keep the exercise short enough to repeat."],["Child’s signal → adult’s response","A toddler points toward a passing truck. Look at it too, name it, and give the child a chance to respond. The exchange can be a shared learning moment without an elaborate educational script. Children communicate through gestures and expressions before they can use sentences. Caregivers also need rest and support; missing an occasional signal does not make someone a bad parent. Look for opportunities to be present during the routines that already belong to family life."],["Price ≠ useful value","A discounted pair of socks may be a worthwhile purchase if you need it. Buying more than your household can use adds storage and sorting even if each transaction is cheap. Ask whether you already own a substitute, when the item will be used and what else your time or money could support. Recording purchases makes repeated decisions easier to examine. A small price is one feature of a good deal, not proof that the purchase is worthwhile."],["Principle → repeated practice","A person can sincerely value patience and still become impatient when tired. That ordinary moment is a meaningful test of conduct. Marcus Aurelius used private reflections to remind himself of principles he wanted to practice. Before a likely difficulty, choose a response in advance. Later, examine what happened without inventing excuses or turning one mistake into a judgment of your entire identity. The next small action is another opportunity to put a principle into practice."],["Movement → sustainable routine","An active routine need not begin with an ambitious training program. Walking and other manageable activities can contribute to an active week. Brief movement breaks may fit a day that otherwise involves prolonged sitting. Activity should suit your abilities and health, with gradual increases when appropriate. A plan must survive ordinary working days as well as enthusiastic weekends. Consult the public-health guidance below for activity recommendations and consider professional advice when health conditions affect your options."],["Belief ↔ evidence","Imagine someone buying an expensive item and later discovering a cheaper alternative. They might emphasize its advantages and dismiss every criticism to protect their confidence in the original decision. Yet defending a choice is not automatically irrational; good reasons may exist. Ask what evidence would change your mind and whether you would apply the same standard to someone else's purchase. Intellectual ability is most useful when it is applied to testing our own conclusions."],["Existing idea + new connection","Learn opportunity cost by connecting it with a familiar household budget. Explain what becomes unavailable when you choose one of two purchases. The new concept gains a meaningful relationship to something you understand. Comparisons also have limits: an analogy can mislead when it ignores important differences. Whenever a new idea reminds you of an old one, identify both a similarity and a difference. A useful connection explains the subject rather than merely linking two memorable words."],["243 Earth days | 225 Earth days","Venus rotates relative to distant stars about once every 243 Earth days, while its orbit takes about 225. It also rotates in the opposite direction to most planets. A solar day, measured from one sunrise to the next, is a different quantity and is shorter than that rotation period. The distinction matters because the word day can have several definitions. Our familiar experience of Earth's calendar is not a universal template for other worlds."],["Listen → understand → answer","A conversation can become an argument before either person understands the other. James encourages attentiveness and restraint in speech and anger. This does not prevent correcting errors or speaking firmly. Try paraphrasing the other person's concern before giving your answer. If the paraphrase is wrong, invite correction. You may still disagree, but you will be addressing the position actually expressed rather than the assumptions you brought into the conversation."],["Public image | Private conduct","Imagine doing careful work that receives no recognition while someone else receives praise for a visible achievement. The Stoic distinction invites you to consider whether your work remains worthwhile without applause. Reputation can matter in professional relationships, because trust affects others. But it depends partly on judgments beyond your control. Avoid making recognition the sole measure of a sound decision. Ask whether the action would remain defensible even if no one knew you performed it."],["Notice → share attention","During a walk, a child becomes fascinated by a leaf rather than the destination the adult has planned. Briefly sharing that attention creates an opportunity for conversation. Describe its color or let the child point again. The goal is not to convert every outing into a lesson; play and unstructured exploration matter too. Following a child's interest occasionally creates an interaction that begins with curiosity rather than an adult's instruction."],["Income → obligations → choices","Start with real expenses rather than an idealized picture of your lifestyle. List recurring obligations, occasional costs and goals that need funding. Then decide what remains available for flexible spending. A budget is not a prediction that every month will go perfectly; unexpected costs are precisely why room for adjustment matters. Revisit the plan when responsibilities change. Its usefulness comes from making a choice before the pressure of an actual purchase arrives."],["Small action → consistency","A ten-minute walk may feel modest beside an ideal exercise program, but it can fit into a busy day. Starting at a manageable level reveals when and where activity is realistic. Small sessions can contribute to an active week, although they are not a substitute for building toward appropriate activity levels if health permits. Consider not only what you can accomplish today, but what you can reasonably repeat under normal circumstances."],["Persuasive presentation ≠ sound argument","An impressive presentation can make a claim feel convincing without making it more accurate. Before sharing an article, separate its style from its evidence. Does the source support the actual conclusion, or a narrower claim? Are other explanations ignored? A polished speaker may be knowledgeable, and an unpolished speaker may be mistaken. Appearance alone cannot settle the question either way. Examine the reasoning beneath the presentation and be willing to suspend judgment if evidence is insufficient."],["Imperial power | Private reflection","Meditations reads less like a public manifesto than a conversation with oneself. An emperor repeatedly reminds himself how to meet frustration and human weakness. The short entries can appear repetitive because ethical problems recur in ordinary life. Rather than collecting isolated quotations, read several entries and ask what concern connects them. The historical distance matters: the text comes from a very different society, yet many of its questions about conduct remain recognizable."],["Pluto: same world, new category","Pluto did not physically change when the International Astronomical Union adopted its planet definition in 2006. The classification changed. Pluto shares its orbital region with other objects, one reason it fails the definition's orbit-clearing criterion. It remains a complex world with moons and an interesting surface. Scientific categories help organize observations, and discoveries can reveal a need for clearer categories. Changing a label does not make the object itself less worthy of study."],["Question → evidence → explanation","Before reading a chapter, ask what you hope to understand by the end. If the topic is memory, ask why recognition is easier than recall in some situations. As you read, seek the author's answer and the reasoning behind it. Revise the question if the chapter reveals a more important issue. This gives your reading direction without requiring you to highlight everything. Remain open to findings that do not fit your initial questions."],["Knowledge → judgment → conduct","A person can remember many principles without applying them when a real choice is difficult. Biblical wisdom literature often directs attention to judgment and conduct. Distinguish what a passage describes from what it recommends, and notice the circumstances it addresses. Identify one concrete decision the teaching might illuminate. Avoid treating every proverb as an unconditional prediction: wisdom sayings can describe general patterns without guaranteeing the outcome of every particular decision."],["Emotion → reflection → response","Anger can alert you to an injustice or a crossed boundary. It can also narrow attention and make a hurried reply seem necessary. A brief delay helps distinguish what occurred from what you assume someone intended. This does not require denying the emotion or accepting mistreatment. A clear, firm response may communicate a boundary better than an insult. If a situation is unsafe, prioritize safety rather than assuming restraint requires staying in harm's way."],["Sun | planets | smaller bodies","A model showing only the Sun and eight planets omits moons, asteroids, comets and other objects. Some smaller bodies preserve clues about the conditions in which the solar system formed. This helps explain why scientific missions study objects that appear tiny or remote. The planets are familiar landmarks, not a complete inventory. A model can make a subject approachable, but the simplification should never be mistaken for the full complexity of the thing being studied."],["Avoidance → relief → repetition","Suppose postponing an unpleasant task produces immediate relief. That relief may increase the likelihood of postponing the next similar task. Negative reinforcement refers to removing something aversive in a way that increases behavior; it is not punishment. The same process can support useful behavior in other circumstances. If avoidance creates problems later, consider a manageable first step and change the conditions that make beginning the task unusually difficult."],["Missed moment → reconnect","No caregiver responds ideally to every expression or request. Daily life includes interruptions, fatigue and misunderstandings. Warm, responsive interactions over time matter more than performing perfectly at every moment. If a child tries to show you something while you are occupied, returning your attention later can still be meaningful. Adults also need support with their own needs. A realistic approach to family life makes space for ordinary human limitations."],["One purchase → recurring pattern","A single inexpensive transaction is easy to forget. Similar transactions across a month may become a noticeable expense or leave a cupboard full of unused things. A spending record turns vague recollection into something you can examine. Group transactions by purpose rather than only by store. A purchase can be cheap and unnecessary, or costly and valuable. The purpose is not to punish small pleasures but to understand how repeated choices relate to your goals."],["Activity ↔ recovery","Physical activity can support better sleep, but it is not a universal solution to every sleep difficulty. Timing, intensity and individual health may affect how a particular session feels. If late exercise leaves you too alert, consider adjusting its timing rather than abandoning activity altogether. Persistent sleep difficulties deserve appropriate medical advice. A sustainable exercise routine should protect opportunities for recovery instead of replacing rest with ever more training."],["Recognition | Recall","Seeing a familiar definition can produce a feeling of knowing it. Producing an explanation without a prompt is a different challenge. Try explaining a concept to someone who has never encountered it and compare your account with the original. You may know the label but not the mechanism. Recognition is useful in its own right; recall and explanation give additional information about your learning. Use both rather than assuming one proves the other."],["Plan → setback → adaptation","A delayed appointment or unexpected obligation can disrupt a carefully planned day. Losing the original plan does not eliminate every worthwhile action. Separate the outcome you hoped for from the choices now available. A Stoic perspective asks how to respond well within the changed circumstances. It does not require pretending the setback is pleasant. Sometimes the responsible action is to reschedule, ask for help or accept that rest is more useful than forcing the original timetable."],["Foundation → visible choices","A foundation is usually less visible than the structure it supports. The biblical image invites reflection on what underlies a person's decisions when circumstances become difficult. Read the passage in context and consider the distinction between hearing a teaching and practicing it. This is an ethical and theological reflection, not a claim that faithful people avoid suffering. Ask whether the commitments you profess are visible in the ordinary decisions of your life."],["One planet → many worlds","Earth's familiar Moon may make one satellite seem normal, but some planets have none and others have extensive moon systems. The giant planets have particularly rich systems, and individual moons can differ greatly in their geology and other characteristics. Counts change as astronomers discover and confirm new objects, making fixed lists vulnerable to becoming outdated. A planetary system can contain a remarkable variety of worlds within its shared journey around the Sun."],["Empire | Everyday conduct","Marcus Aurelius occupied one of the most powerful positions in Rome, yet his surviving reflections often examine mundane frustrations and personal obligations. The contrast helps make the book interesting. Historical readers should not assume that every ethical ideal expressed in a notebook was perfectly embodied in its author's political life. The work is evidence of his reflections, not a complete biography or defense of imperial government. Study it alongside historical context."],["Uncertain reward → persistent response","If a mailbox occasionally contains an anticipated letter, you might keep checking even after several unsuccessful attempts. The pattern of consequences can affect how behavior persists. Reinforcement schedules are useful for understanding frequent checking, but not every instance of digital use has the same explanation. Social connection, convenience and boredom also matter. For learning, aim for experiences that are consistently worthwhile and allow you to stop, rather than manufacturing unpredictable prizes."],["Shared attention → language","When a child reaches toward a cup, naming it connects a word to something meaningful in the moment. The child may answer with a sound, gesture or word. Daily routines provide opportunities for these exchanges without elaborate materials. Follow the child's pace rather than demanding an educational performance. Language development varies, and concerns about a particular child's progress are best discussed with a qualified professional rather than judged by an isolated anecdote."],["Present money → future purpose","Savings are easily overlooked when treated as whatever remains at the end of a month. Give a specific goal a place in the budget so its competition with present spending is visible. Urgent obligations or debt may need priority, and appropriate amounts depend on a household's circumstances. Name the purpose, estimate what is affordable and review the plan. The objective is to make the trade-off explicit rather than impose a single rule on every household."],["Explain → identify gaps → revise","Try teaching someone why the Moon has phases without relying on a vague phrase like 'because of the Sun.' You must explain what is illuminated and how the viewing angle changes. Teaching exposes missing links in a chain of reasoning. A short explanation can reveal whether you understand the mechanism or only recognize terminology. Compare your explanation with a credible source afterward; simplicity is valuable only when it preserves accuracy."],["Ambition | Service","Wanting to accomplish something differs from wanting to be admired for it. The New Testament teaching on humility invites attention to how your goals affect other people. A promotion, business or academic achievement can serve a worthwhile purpose, yet it may also become a measure of status at the expense of relationships. Ask who benefits if a goal succeeds and what you would not sacrifice to achieve it. Read the teaching within its theological context."],["Rocky Earth | Gas giant Jupiter","Earth has a rocky composition and a solid surface, while Jupiter is dominated by hydrogen and helium and lacks an ordinary solid surface like Earth's. The distinction shows why planetary size alone cannot explain what a world is made of. Formation history and physical conditions matter as well. A mission to explore these worlds would require different instruments and approaches. The categories astronomers use have practical consequences for scientific investigation."],["Obstacle → useful next step","A complicated goal can seem impossible when you try to solve every future difficulty simultaneously. Identify one action that is possible and relevant. For a book, it may mean opening a chapter; for a household project, gathering missing information. This does not eliminate the need for planning or coordination. It prevents uncertainty about an entire outcome from blocking every useful action. After the first step, reconsider what you now know."],["Category ≠ complete explanation","Categories make a complex world easier to navigate, but borderline cases expose their limitations. A quiet person in a meeting might be thoughtful, nervous, tired or simply uninterested. Assigning a single label too quickly hides plausible alternatives. Categories are useful starting points for investigation rather than final verdicts, particularly when interpreting other people's actions. Ask what evidence would distinguish the explanations you are considering before committing to a judgment."],["Single effort → durable routine","A demanding workout cannot substitute for a pattern of activity that fits ordinary responsibilities. A useful plan may include more challenging sessions and lighter days, adjusted for health, recovery and ability. Progress is not always linear; interruptions are normal. Rather than treating a missed session as a complete failure, identify a safe way to resume. A plan's durability matters more than how impressive every individual session appears."],["Quotation → paragraph → argument","A memorable quotation may be the beginning of a worthwhile reading experience, not the end. An author could be responding to an earlier argument, addressing a particular audience or making a distinction that disappears in isolation. Read the surrounding paragraphs and identify the question being answered. Different translations can express the same passage differently. Context helps protect against false attributions and using a genuine quotation to support a position the author did not hold."],["Different schools | Different questions","Psychology developed through debates about what should be studied and how. Some approaches emphasized conscious experience, others observable behavior, and later approaches investigated mental processes with different methods. These traditions disagreed about what counted as an explanation. When reading a theory, ask what question it was designed to address, what evidence supports it and what remains unresolved. Its history helps prevent the assumption that one school explains every aspect of human behavior."],["Words → relationship consequences","A sentence spoken in irritation can alter the direction of an entire conversation. A gentler response may leave room for understanding, whereas an insult can transform disagreement into a struggle over dignity. The proverb describes a general pattern rather than guaranteeing that every hostile person will respond to kindness. Sometimes a firm correction or boundary is necessary. Choose language that serves truth and the relationship rather than merely satisfying the desire to retaliate."],["Icy nucleus → coma and tails","Far from the Sun, a comet can be a relatively inactive object. Nearer the Sun, heating releases gas and dust that may produce a coma and conspicuous tails. The tails are shaped by solar radiation and the solar wind; they do not simply trail behind like smoke from a vehicle. These visible changes reveal something about the material in the nucleus. A comet's appearance reflects its interaction with its surroundings."],["Needs | Meaningful wants","A book, family outing or hobby can express a genuine value even if it is not essential for survival. A spending plan distinguishes obligations from flexible choices without labeling all enjoyment wasteful. Compare a purchase with the other things your money could support, including future obligations. Reviewing real spending makes the trade-offs easier to see. The best budget is one realistic enough to follow, not necessarily the most restrictive."],["Praise → observe the effect","One person may appreciate praise for reading while another finds it embarrassing. The name of a consequence does not establish its effect. In behavioral analysis, reinforcement is identified by an increase in behavior under the relevant conditions. If you are developing a learning habit, observe which features of the experience actually support it. A satisfying story or discovery may be more useful than a congratulatory message or a progress badge."],["Event | Interpretation","Suppose someone fails to answer a message. One interpretation is deliberate disrespect; another is that the person is occupied or did not see it. The event alone does not establish which is true. Before reacting, consider what evidence could distinguish the possibilities. This does not require ignoring wrongdoing or repeated patterns. It encourages examining your assumptions before allowing an untested interpretation to determine your response."],["Facts → relationships → understanding","Connect memory, motivation and reading habits. A rewarding reading experience may encourage repetition, while deliberately recalling what you read can reveal gaps in understanding. These are different processes with different functions. Linking them should not collapse them into one explanation. A useful knowledge network contains relationships that can be justified. When saving an idea, identify one connection to something you already know and one question that remains open."]];
CONTENT.forEach((p,i)=>{[p.visual,p.extra]=EDITORIAL[i]});
const MEDIA={2:['Audiobook · human narration','LibriVox — The Enchiridion','https://librivox.org/the-enchiridion-by-epictetus-tr-elizabeth-carter/'],4:['Educational video','NASA — Our World: Moon Phases','https://science.nasa.gov/eclips/videos/moon-phases/'],6:['Educational video','Harvard — 5 Steps for Brain-Building Serve and Return','https://developingchild.harvard.edu/resources/videos/how-to-5-steps-for-brain-building-serve-and-return/'],8:['Audiobook · human narration','LibriVox — Meditations','https://librivox.org/the-meditations-of-marcus-aurelius/'],19:['Audiobook · human narration','LibriVox — Meditations','https://librivox.org/the-meditations-of-marcus-aurelius/'],33:['Audiobook · human narration','LibriVox — Meditations','https://librivox.org/the-meditations-of-marcus-aurelius/'],35:['Educational video','Harvard — Serve and Return Interaction','https://developingchild.harvard.edu/resources/videos/serve-return-interaction-shapes-brain-circuitry/']};
const BETTER_SOURCES={12:['NASA · Venus facts','https://science.nasa.gov/venus/venus-facts/'],20:['NASA · Pluto facts','https://science.nasa.gov/dwarf-planets/pluto/facts/'],46:['NASA · Comets','https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/comets/']};
