for(const [id,revision] of Object.entries({"69":{"body":"Mercury is the innermost planet, but the most intense planetary surface heat belongs to Venus. The apparent contradiction becomes clearer when we consider both incoming energy and the rate at which heat escapes. A planet's surface absorbs sunlight and radiates energy in the infrared. Venus has an enormous atmosphere dominated by carbon dioxide. Its greenhouse effect impedes the escape of thermal radiation from the lower atmosphere and surface. NASA describes surface temperatures around 467 degrees Celsius, despite Venus orbiting farther from the Sun than Mercury.","extra":"Mercury has only an extremely thin exosphere, rather than an atmosphere capable of producing the same greenhouse warming. Its sunlit and nighttime surfaces can therefore experience very different temperatures. Venus, meanwhile, also has reflective clouds, so the amount of sunlight arriving near a planet does not tell us how much its surface absorbs. The final temperature reflects several processes operating together. This comparison is useful when learning to reason scientifically: a relationship such as greater sunlight at smaller orbital distances may be correct, yet insufficient to predict a planet's surface conditions. Venus is an extreme example of why the composition and structure of an atmosphere matter as much as the location of a planet when discussing its climate.","source":"NASA Science · Venus Facts","url":"https://science.nasa.gov/venus/venus-facts/"},"77":{"body":"A person prepares carefully for an examination and wants to succeed. Preparation, the effort made during the examination and the decision to seek feedback are activities the person can undertake. The final result also depends on circumstances and decisions beyond that person's authority. Epictetus begins the Enchiridion by distinguishing our own choices and judgments from many external matters. His advice about desire belongs to a broader Stoic account of freedom: placing one's deepest good entirely in outcomes that others or circumstances can determine makes that good vulnerable to forces outside one's agency.","extra":"This is more demanding than the familiar suggestion to stop worrying. Epictetus asks readers to reconsider what they treat as essential to living well. A modern application might be to pursue a goal seriously while recognizing that success is not guaranteed by effort alone. That does not mean abandoning responsibilities, accepting injustice or refusing assistance. People can often influence conditions that they cannot fully control, and ethical action may require doing so. The distinction helps clarify the difference between pursuing a result, influencing its likelihood and possessing authority over its eventual outcome. Read the opening sections of the linked translation to see how the ancient philosopher connects desire, aversion and judgments to his larger ethical framework."},"78":{"body":"Psalm 1 introduces two contrasting ways of life. In its opening verse, the righteous person refuses the influence of corrupt counsel and conduct. The next verse presents a positive alternative: delight in God's instruction and sustained meditation on it. The psalm is not merely asking the reader to avoid wrongdoing. It describes a life shaped by what the person loves, thinks about and follows. The image then changes to a tree planted beside streams, producing fruit at the proper season. Its stability comes from a dependable source of nourishment rather than from the appearance of strength alone.","extra":"The contrasting image is chaff, the light material separated from grain that can be scattered by wind. The final verses speak of judgment and the different destinations of the righteous and wicked. Within a Christian reading, the contrast invites examination of the influences shaping one's conduct and the place of God's Word in daily life. The tree metaphor should not be reduced to a formula promising wealth, uninterrupted health or freedom from suffering. Other psalms give voice to faithful people experiencing distress. Read the six verses as a complete poem before deciding how to apply it. A useful reflection is to ask what repeatedly nourishes your attention and whether it corresponds to the way of life you profess to follow."},"83":{"body":"Stand near a table and choose an object at its center. Walk around the table while continually facing the object. At the halfway point, you are facing the opposite direction from where you started; after one circuit, you have turned through a full revolution relative to the room. This is a simple model of the Moon's synchronized rotation. The Moon completes one turn relative to distant stars in approximately the same 27.3 days that it takes to orbit Earth. If it did not rotate, observers on Earth would see different sides of it as it moved around its orbit.","extra":"The synchronized motion is the result of long-term tidal interactions, which have altered the Moon's rotation. The near side is not perfectly fixed in our view: small apparent movements called libration reveal additional regions around its edges over time. The far side is also not permanently dark. As the Moon moves around Earth, sunlight illuminates different portions of its surface. At new moon, the hemisphere generally facing Earth is mostly unilluminated while the opposite side receives sunlight. At full moon, the situation is reversed. The key is to separate three ideas: rotation relative to space, orientation relative to Earth and illumination by the Sun. They describe different relationships even though they all contribute to the Moon's familiar appearance."},"84":{"body":"A spacecraft looking at Europa sees an intensely cold world covered in water ice. But scientists have evidence that the exterior may conceal a global ocean. One important clue came from measurements made by NASA's Galileo spacecraft: Europa responded to changes in Jupiter's magnetic environment in a way consistent with an electrically conducting layer inside the moon. Salty liquid water is a plausible explanation for that response. The hypothesis combines observations of the magnetic field with knowledge of Europa's composition and other characteristics; it is not based solely on a photograph of its icy surface.","extra":"Europa's orbit also creates changing tidal forces. As Jupiter's gravity deforms the moon, repeated flexing can convert some mechanical energy into internal heat. This provides a possible explanation for how liquid water persists beneath the ice despite the extremely low temperatures at the surface. A subsurface ocean makes Europa scientifically interesting, but it does not establish that life exists there. Habitability involves the availability of suitable chemistry, energy and environmental conditions; detecting organisms would require additional evidence. NASA's investigations aim to refine our understanding of the moon's ice, interior and surface chemistry. Europa is an example of how scientists infer hidden structures by combining independent observations and testing physical explanations.","source":"NASA Science · Europa Facts","url":"https://science.nasa.gov/jupiter/jupiter-moons/europa/europa-facts/"},"93":{"body":"A child spills a drink and watches an adult respond impatiently. Later, the same adult realizes that the reaction was unfair. A specific apology followed by a calmer attempt to solve the problem gives the child an example of what taking responsibility can look like. This is different from explaining that people should apologize while never demonstrating the behavior oneself. The research framework of observational learning emphasizes that children and adults can acquire information from other people's conduct. What is learned may influence behavior later, even when no immediate imitation is visible.","extra":"For a parent, the important example is not an elaborate speech but a repair that matches the situation. An adult can acknowledge what they did, avoid placing responsibility for their reaction on the child and demonstrate how to respond differently. The child may need time and practice to develop comparable skills. A single apology cannot guarantee that a child will learn a particular lesson, and repeated harmful conduct is not made acceptable by apologies alone. This example is an application of modeling theory rather than a tested universal parenting formula. Consider the pattern children observe over many interactions: how adults handle frustration, keep commitments and respond when they discover that they were wrong."},"95":{"body":"Imagine a household considering a new electric fan. A retailer discounts the model from ₱2,000 to ₱1,600, a reduction of 20%. During the same period, several frequently purchased groceries become more expensive. The discount is real, but it does not establish that the general cost of living has fallen. Inflation describes changes in a broad price measure, while the price of a particular product can respond to changes in its supply, demand, production costs and competition. These two kinds of price movement can occur simultaneously.","extra":"An important distinction concerns what economists call relative prices. A product can become cheaper compared with other goods even when its own money price rises, if the other prices rise more quickly. Suppose an appliance becomes 2% more expensive while a representative basket rises 8%. Relative to that basket, the appliance has become less expensive. This is an illustrative calculation, not a claim about any current market. For a household, the practical question is whether a purchase fits its needs and budget, not merely whether its price moved in an attractive direction. A discount on an unnecessary item cannot offset increased spending on essentials simply by being advertised as savings. Compare like products, quantities and total expenditures before interpreting price changes."},"99":{"body":"Imagine investigating a town celebration that took place a century ago. A newspaper might establish when the event happened and which people were publicly recognized. A photograph might show the arrangement of a procession, clothing or buildings. A popular song might express the hopes or fears that some people associated with the occasion. These sources do not simply repeat the same information in different formats. They offer different kinds of evidence, and each was created for a particular audience and purpose. A historian can use the differences between them to develop new questions.","extra":"The Library of Congress organizes teaching collections that bring together documents of multiple kinds, including maps, newspaper reports, sheet music and photographs. When exploring such a collection, distinguish what the source directly records from what you infer. A newspaper editorial may establish that an argument was published without proving its factual claims. A posed photograph may show a chosen representation of a group rather than an ordinary moment. A song may express one community's perspective without speaking for everyone. Ask who produced each item, when, for whom and why. Where the evidence conflicts, do not rush to choose the account that best fits your expectations. The disagreement itself may reveal how people experienced or represented the past.","source":"Library of Congress · Primary Source Sets","url":"https://www.loc.gov/programs/teachers/classroom-materials/primary-source-sets/"},"100":{"body":"Suppose a colleague responds to a long message with a single word. You immediately interpret the reply as contempt. Yet the message itself does not tell you whether the colleague was angry, hurried, distracted or simply concise. In the opening of the Enchiridion, Epictetus recommends examining appearances before treating them as reliable accounts of reality. His Stoic approach connects that examination to the difference between our own judgments and choices and circumstances that do not belong entirely to us. The exercise is not a demand to pretend that the reply felt pleasant.","extra":"A modern practical application is to distinguish an observation, an interpretation and a proposed action. The observation is that the reply contained one word. The interpretation is that it was intended as disrespect. The action might be to request clarification or postpone an unnecessary confrontation. A second look may confirm the initial concern, but it can also reveal other explanations. This is an application of the ancient ethical teaching, not a claim that every emotional response is consciously chosen or that wrongdoing should be ignored. Genuine misconduct can require a firm response. The purpose is to let evidence and considered judgment guide that response rather than allowing an uncertain first impression to settle the matter by itself."},"142":{"body":"The Library of Congress preserves a photographic print cataloged as a Filipino family portrait, showing ten people posed on a lawn. The catalog credits photographer Perley Fremont Rockett and dates the photograph to approximately 1899. The image offers a starting point for studying family representation during a period of major political change in the Philippines. A historian can ask questions about clothing, the arrangement of the group and the setting. The answers may suggest how the people in the photograph wished to be represented or how the photographer chose to present them.","extra":"A portrait does not by itself establish the identities, occupations or economic status of everyone pictured. The catalog title and date also deserve scrutiny, especially when a date is supplied in brackets rather than documented precisely. Compare the image with other evidence from the period before drawing conclusions about a wider society. This method applies equally to photographs in a family's own archive. Recording names, relationships, approximate dates and locations can make otherwise ordinary photographs more useful to future generations. Preserve the original alongside its digital copy and distinguish known facts from family recollections or guesses. A photograph becomes a stronger historical source when the information needed to interpret it survives with it.","source":"Library of Congress · Filipino family portrait, circa 1899","url":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/collection/cph/item/93506800/","photo":["https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/pnp/cph/3c00000/3c07000/3c07800/3c07896r.jpg","Cataloged portrait of a Filipino family posed on a lawn","Perley Fremont Rockett · Library of Congress · no known publication restrictions","https://www.loc.gov/pictures/collection/cph/item/93506800/"]}})){const p=CONTENT.find(x=>x.id===Number(id));Object.assign(p,revision,{editorialReview:{date:'2026-11-27',status:'source-aligned revision',scope:'Main reference inspected or replaced with accessible authoritative source; independently written explanations and examples. Not independent peer review.'}})}

// v2.0 RC7: fourth source-aligned editorial depth pass.
for(const [id,revision] of Object.entries({"60":{"body":"Saturn's rings look like a single spectacular feature when seen through a small telescope, but the system includes regions with different properties. Outside the prominent bright rings lies the diffuse E ring. NASA's Cassini mission established a connection between this ring and Enceladus, one of Saturn's icy moons. Material escaping from the moon contributes particles to the surrounding environment as Enceladus travels around Saturn. Instead of regarding the ring and moon as entirely separate objects, scientists can investigate how the material released by one helps sustain the other.","extra":"This discovery illustrates how a planetary system can change through exchanges among its components. Particles in orbit may encounter moons, respond to gravitational influences and be redistributed or lost. A ring observed today need not contain exactly the same material that occupied it long ago. NASA also describes interactions in which moons remove material from rings rather than supplying it. The direction of exchange depends on the physical circumstances. The particular connection between Enceladus and the E ring should not be generalized into an explanation for the origin of every ring around Saturn. The strength of the Cassini result is the specific observational relationship it established. Looking for such relationships turns a familiar picture of a planet surrounded by rings into a question about ongoing physical processes."},"88":{"body":"Consider a cell surrounded by water containing dissolved substances that cannot freely cross its membrane. If the effective concentration of those substances differs from that inside the cell, water can move across the membrane in response to the imbalance. This process is osmosis. In a simplified example, placing an animal cell in a solution with a much lower concentration of nonpenetrating solutes can cause water to enter it, producing swelling. A solution with a higher effective concentration can instead draw water out. The movement does not require the cell to decide consciously which direction is preferable.","extra":"Living organisms cannot rely on a membrane to maintain suitable conditions by itself. They regulate the quantities of water and dissolved substances in their internal fluids through mechanisms collectively described as osmoregulation. Transport proteins help control the movement of ions, and some transport processes require cellular energy. The ability of a solute to cross a membrane matters, so comparing total concentrations without considering permeability can be misleading. Different organisms face different challenges depending on their environments and cellular structures. A diagram showing water moving in one direction is therefore a useful starting point rather than a complete model of physiological regulation. The central idea is that biological systems must manage ordinary physical processes to preserve the internal conditions on which their functions depend."},"90":{"body":"Imagine a fictional population of insects in which an inherited coloration differs among individuals. Under one set of environmental conditions, individuals with one variant may leave more offspring than those with another. If the differences are inherited, the composition of the population can change over successive generations. Natural selection refers to this process of differential reproductive success associated with heritable variation. It does not mean that an individual develops a needed inherited feature by wishing for it or by encountering a challenge during its lifetime.","extra":"The relationship between a trait and reproductive success depends on the environment. The same coloration might provide a benefit in one setting and little benefit in another. A trait can also involve trade-offs: an advantage in one aspect of life need not make its bearer universally superior. In addition to selection, population change can involve mutation, migration and chance processes. Consequently, observing that a trait became common does not establish by itself which mechanism produced the change. Researchers examine inheritance, environmental conditions and reproductive outcomes to test explanations. This population-level framework helps distinguish an evolutionary explanation from a story that merely assumes every present-day characteristic must have arisen for a particular purpose. The fictional example is intended to make the mechanism understandable, not to report a documented study of an insect species."},"97":{"body":"Imagine reconstructing the journey of a relative who moved from one town to another decades ago. A conventional timeline might tell you the order of the moves. A map could reveal whether those locations follow a railway, coastline or other geographical feature. Add a dated letter or family photograph to each place, and the journey gains personal and historical context. Story maps combine spatial information with narrative and other records in this way. The Library of Congress maintains examples that draw on materials from its collections to explain historical and geographical topics.","extra":"The map is also an interpretation. Choosing which places to include, which route to draw and where a story begins can influence what readers conclude. If the only evidence places a person in two towns, a line between them should not imply that the exact route is known. A useful story map separates documented locations from estimated connections and makes the underlying sources available. Historical boundaries and place names can change as well; a modern map should not silently stand in for conditions in an earlier period. Try this method with a family history, local event or migration account. Start with the records rather than drawing a convincing route first. The value of the finished map lies in how clearly it connects geographical claims to evidence.","source":"Library of Congress · Geography and Map Reading Room: Story Maps","url":"https://www.loc.gov/research-centers/geography-and-map/researcher-resources/story-maps/"},"116":{"body":"Vincent van Gogh's 1887 Sunflowers at the Metropolitan Museum of Art offers an opportunity to look beyond the familiar vase compositions associated with his name. Here the cut flower heads occupy much of the visual field. The arrangement encourages the viewer to examine their surfaces, the contrast between petals and centers, and the direction in which the blooms lie. The museum places this work among four sunflower still lifes created in Paris during the same period. Seeing it alongside those related works reveals how an artist can explore one subject repeatedly without producing identical images.","extra":"A still life allows the painter to change the arrangement, scale, viewpoint and relationship between objects and background. These choices influence what becomes visually important. The flower head may be treated as a recognizable plant, but its structure also offers opportunities to experiment with color and marks made by a brush. Try comparing this painting with another version before reading a critical interpretation. Note where your attention goes first, how the edges of objects are handled and whether the background seems to separate or connect them. These observations are starting points for an interpretation rather than proof of what the artist privately intended. The museum's account supplies historical context; the visible work supplies evidence for your own examination of its composition."},"118":{"body":"Jacques-Louis David's 1787 painting The Death of Socrates gives its philosophical subject a striking visual organization. The central figure sits upright while others display anguish and uncertainty. His raised hand draws attention toward the argument he is making, while his other hand approaches the cup. The surrounding figures help the viewer notice the contrast between his composure and their distress. The painting does not merely illustrate a conversation: it proposes a way of understanding the philosopher's commitment to his beliefs. The Metropolitan Museum of Art places the work within the neoclassical tradition and its interest in classical examples of moral conduct.","extra":"Notice how the artist includes Plato near the bed, although Plato was not present at the death according to the account preserved in the Phaedo. His presence connects the scene to the text through which later readers encounter it. This deliberate departure from the historical account demonstrates that visual accuracy and interpretive meaning are different concerns. Compare the painting with the dialogue itself and ask which aspects of the story each medium emphasizes. The philosopher's composure, the reactions of his companions and the arrangement of the room all guide interpretation. Neither the painting nor this reading of it settles whether the philosophical position is correct. The artwork is an invitation to examine the argument and the values expressed through its composition."},"120":{"body":"NASA's photograph of Jupiter's Great Red Spot combines two JunoCam images acquired on February 12, 2019. Image processor Kevin M. Gill assembled them into an enhanced-color view. At the time, Juno was about 70,000 kilometers above the cloud tops, and the image could distinguish structures approximately 50 kilometers across. The spacecraft's viewpoint reveals swirling features within the spot and adjacent regions that are difficult to distinguish in a small telescope on Earth. These features belong to the atmosphere rather than to a solid landmark on Jupiter's surface.","extra":"Think of a persistent whirlpool in a stream. Its recognizable form can remain while water enters and leaves it. In a broadly similar sense, a storm can retain a recognizable pattern without consisting of the same gas throughout its existence. The analogy is limited because Jupiter's atmosphere has its own complex dynamics. The photograph captures conditions at particular moments and does not demonstrate that every observed feature persists indefinitely. Enhanced colors also deserve attention: the displayed image is a processed representation of measurements rather than necessarily the scene as a nearby human observer would see it. Reading NASA's image description helps separate the date, resolution and processing information from the physical interpretation. This is a useful habit whenever a striking scientific photograph appears without its original caption."},"122":{"body":"Consider two stars whose light takes ten years and one hundred years respectively to reach Earth. An observer looking at them on the same evening receives information from different periods in their histories. The telescope does not send the observer into the past; it collects radiation that has been traveling since an earlier event. This distinction becomes extraordinarily important when astronomers observe distant galaxies. Hubble's deep-field images reveal objects whose light has traveled for immense periods of time, enabling researchers to investigate earlier stages of cosmic history.","extra":"A deep-field image is consequently not a snapshot of everything as it exists at one common moment. Different objects can be represented at very different epochs. Astronomers compare populations of galaxies and use additional measurements, including their spectra, to investigate how galaxies have changed. These comparisons require care: a faint galaxy is not automatically an older galaxy, because its brightness depends on more than distance. The expansion of space also complicates the relationship between travel time and present-day distance. The useful insight is that seeing further can provide access to earlier information, but interpreting that information requires physical models. A deep field gives scientists a collection of observations from which to reconstruct history, not a complete recording of a single galaxy's life."},"128":{"body":"A careful reader of the 1734 map of the Philippine islands preserved by the Library of Congress can find the name Nicolás de la Cruz Bagay in its lower portion. The catalog identifies Bagay as an engraver associated with the chart drawn by Pedro Murillo Velarde and published in Manila. This credit matters because a finished map is more than a set of geographical observations. Engraving transforms a design into a surface capable of producing printed copies. The maker must preserve relationships between lines, lettering and illustrations so that the result communicates recognizable information.","extra":"The map therefore invites us to consider the people behind its production, rather than assuming that the name most commonly attached to it describes the entire undertaking. Its inscriptions and surrounding illustrations can be studied alongside the geographical information they frame. Ask how the skills of artists, engravers and printers helped determine the artifact that survived. The catalog's documented credit is firmer evidence of Bagay's role than an unsupported assumption about which details he personally designed. At the same time, the chart's colonial context calls for attention to whose knowledge and perspectives appear in it. A map can document both the geography people sought to represent and the collaborative work necessary to make that representation circulate.","source":"Library of Congress · 1734 Carta hydrographica y chorographica de las Yslas Filipinas","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2013585226/"},"145":{"body":"A young child watches an adult zip a jacket. The child may recognize that the two sides must be brought together and that a small piece must move upward, yet still be unable to align the fastener or apply the necessary force. Such an experience illustrates the difference between acquiring information and demonstrating a skill. In the observational-learning framework discussed by OpenStax, a person's ability to reproduce an observed behavior depends on more than exposure to an example. The learner must attend to the relevant details, retain them and have the capacity and motivation to act.","extra":"For a parent, this suggests dividing a difficult routine into manageable steps. Show the action slowly, allow the child to attempt one part and provide help with the portions beyond their present ability. An immediate failure may reflect coordination, attention or unfamiliarity with the materials rather than a complete absence of learning. Repeated demonstrations alone, however, cannot guarantee mastery. Practice provides a chance to connect remembered information with physical action. This is a practical application of the learning framework, not a universal schedule for when children should acquire particular abilities. Development varies, and adults should adjust expectations to the learner and task. A useful teaching question is not only whether the child can complete a routine today, but which part of it the child now understands."}})){const p=CONTENT.find(x=>x.id===Number(id));Object.assign(p,revision,{editorialReview:{date:'2026-11-27',status:'source-aligned revision',scope:'Eight main reference pages retrieved; two blocked LOC blogs replaced with relevant indexed LOC collection pages. Independent explanations and examples, not independent peer review.'}})}

