for(const [id,revision] of Object.entries({"63":{"body":"Two siblings remember a family gathering differently. One recalls a relative arriving before dinner; the other is certain the arrival happened afterward. Neither needs to be lying. Memory is not a preserved video that the mind simply plays back. Details may be incompletely encoded in the first place, and later information can influence the reconstruction of an experience. OpenStax describes several ways memory can go wrong, including source confusion and the influence of misleading information. A detail learned in a later conversation can eventually feel like part of the original experience.","extra":"An important distinction is between the strength of a person's conviction and the evidence supporting a particular detail. Confidence can be informative in some circumstances, but it cannot establish accuracy on its own. Imagine checking the time of the gathering against a photograph with reliable metadata or a contemporary message. Each additional record has its own limitations, but independent evidence can narrow the possibilities. When asking someone about an event, begin with an open invitation to describe what they remember rather than supplying an expected answer. Separate what the person directly observed from what they heard afterward. This does not mean that every recollection is suspect or useless. It means that the appropriate degree of certainty depends on the circumstances, the detail in question and the available corroboration."},"64":{"body":"Imagine learning to ride a bicycle from an illustrated manual. You might memorize instructions about balance and steering without being able to stay upright. Conversely, an experienced cyclist can navigate a corner while finding it difficult to describe the precise movements involved. Psychology distinguishes explicit or declarative memory, which includes facts and personally experienced events, from implicit memory, which includes acquired procedures. The categories help explain why being able to describe an action and being able to perform it are different achievements. A skill may become increasingly automatic with practice, even though conscious attention is useful while learning it.","extra":"Consider someone learning to type. They can study the keyboard layout, but finding the keys without looking requires repeated attempts. A useful learning sequence is to understand a small part of the task, perform it, notice the result and adjust. Explicit knowledge can guide the practice, and successful practice can reveal where the learner's explanation is incomplete. These forms of memory are not opponents: competent performance often depends on both. A person who practices a musical instrument may also study notation and harmony to make better decisions about interpretation. When planning to learn a new skill, decide what you should be able to explain and what you should be able to demonstrate. Test each separately. Reading about a skill is valuable, but the ability to act is not automatically produced by reading."},"68":{"body":"Suppose Ana can produce eight baskets of vegetables or four repaired tools in a day, while Ben can produce six baskets or two repaired tools. Ana has an absolute advantage in both activities. But producing one repaired tool costs Ana two baskets of vegetables, whereas it costs Ben three baskets. For one basket of vegetables, Ana gives up half a repaired tool and Ben gives up one-third. Ana therefore has the comparative advantage in repairs; Ben has it in vegetables. Their different relative costs create a possible basis for cooperation even though Ana is faster at both jobs.","extra":"If Ana repairs four tools and Ben grows six baskets, they can exchange one tool for two and a half baskets. In the simplified example, Ana receives more vegetables than the two she would sacrifice to produce the tool, while Ben pays fewer than the three baskets he would sacrifice to repair one himself. The exchange is potentially beneficial to both relative to their own production costs. This model assumes constant tradeoffs, divisible output, and feasible exchange. Real specialization can be limited by demand, transport costs, risk, changing technology and unequal bargaining power. The lesson is not that any particular trade arrangement must be desirable. It is that productivity alone does not tell us which activity a person should specialize in: the alternative output forgone also matters.","source":"OpenStax Principles of Economics 3e · Absolute and Comparative Advantage","url":"https://openstax.org/books/principles-economics-3e/pages/33-1-absolute-and-comparative-advantage"},"73":{"body":"A sapling becomes a substantial tree without removing an equivalent mass of earth from its surroundings. Much of its new dry material is assembled from carbon obtained from the air. In photosynthesis, leaves use light energy to make organic compounds from carbon dioxide and water. The plant can then use these compounds to build cellulose and other materials, or break them down to support cellular activity. Sunlight provides energy rather than supplying the carbon atoms. Water and mineral nutrients absorbed by roots are still essential, and water contributes heavily to the mass of living, undried tissue.","extra":"This distinction between matter and energy explains an otherwise puzzling observation. A tree does not grow merely because sunlight shines on it, and fertilizer cannot replace the carbon dioxide required to build its tissues. Think of a greenhouse: light, water, suitable temperature, mineral nutrition and access to carbon dioxide all influence plant growth. A shortage of one requirement can limit growth even when others are abundant. The tree also carries out respiration, returning some carbon to the atmosphere, so the carbon retained in its tissues reflects more than its rate of photosynthesis alone. An interesting way to study the process is to compare what enters a plant, what leaves it, and what remains stored. The apparent solidity of wood conceals a continuous exchange of matter and energy between living organisms and their environment."},"75":{"body":"A diary written on the day of a historical event can provide direct evidence of what its author saw, believed or felt. It does not necessarily establish what happened in every other part of the community. The term primary source describes a relationship to the question being investigated, not a guarantee that every statement is true. A participant may have limited access to information, a strong interest in presenting events in a particular way, or an ordinary misunderstanding. A later historical study may synthesize many independent documents, although it too must be evaluated for its reasoning and evidence.","extra":"Consider a letter claiming that everyone welcomed a new policy. The letter demonstrates that its author made that claim; it does not establish unanimous approval. Look for who wrote it, when it was produced, who was intended to read it and what other evidence exists. The Library of Congress teaching framework encourages examining observable details, considering their possible significance and developing questions for further inquiry. Use those steps to keep evidence separate from interpretation. A newspaper, a private letter and an official record may each illuminate different aspects of the same event. Conflicting accounts can themselves reveal differences in perspective. The goal is neither automatic belief nor automatic skepticism. It is to establish which conclusions the available evidence actually supports."},"79":{"body":"Psalm 23 begins with the image of God as a shepherd. The speaker describes rest, restoration and guidance along right paths. The scene then changes: the speaker walks through a place associated with deep darkness and danger. The reason for confidence is not the disappearance of every threat but God's presence. The language also becomes more personal at this point. Earlier lines describe what the Lord does; in the valley, the speaker addresses God directly. The shepherd's equipment becomes an image of comfort in the midst of uncertainty. The poem makes room for both peace and hardship within the experience of trusting God.","extra":"The final verses move toward the imagery of a generous host, a prepared table and an overflowing cup. Even the presence of enemies does not remove the speaker's confidence in God's care. Read together, these scenes resist an interpretation that faith guarantees a comfortable life without trouble. Christians can understand the Psalm as an invitation to trust God's character while continuing through difficult circumstances, rather than as a timetable for when those circumstances will end. In practice, prayer, wise action and accepting help can coexist with trust. Read the entire Psalm slowly, attending to the transitions between pasture, valley and hospitality. Ask what changes in the surroundings and what remains constant in the speaker's relationship with God. The shape of the poem conveys more than any one familiar line can communicate alone."},"82":{"body":"A total solar eclipse depends on an alignment in which the Moon passes between Earth and the Sun and completely covers the Sun's visible disk for observers in part of its shadow. The innermost shadow, called the umbra, reaches only a comparatively narrow region on Earth's surface. People outside it may be inside the broader penumbra and see only part of the Sun covered. The Moon's orbit is tilted relative to Earth's orbital plane, which explains why every new moon does not produce a solar eclipse. The relative apparent sizes of the Sun and Moon also matter: when the Moon appears too small to cover the Sun fully, an annular eclipse can occur instead.","extra":"The same event can therefore look dramatically different from locations only a modest distance apart. Eclipse maps combine the predicted movements of Earth and Moon with the geometry of their shadows. They illustrate why astronomical predictions must include the observer's location rather than just a date. Safe viewing is essential: use certified solar viewers meeting ISO 12312-2 for direct viewing whenever any bright portion of the Sun is visible. Ordinary sunglasses are not adequate. Only during the brief complete totality of a total eclipse can direct viewing without such protection be safe, and protection must be restored before the bright solar surface returns. Never look through unfiltered binoculars or telescopes. NASA's guidance explains these distinctions in more detail."},"96":{"body":"Imagine a market where a storm damages part of the vegetable harvest. With fewer vegetables available at previous prices, sellers may raise prices as buyers compete for supplies. Buyers may choose substitutes, while producers may have an incentive to bring additional vegetables to market when that is feasible. The resulting price conveys information about the interaction of supply and demand. It can help coordinate decisions among people who do not know each other's circumstances. But a price is not a measurement of every characteristic that makes something valuable. It reflects willingness and ability to pay under the conditions of that market.","extra":"Consider a product whose manufacturing process pollutes a river. If the cost of that pollution is borne by downstream residents rather than included in the producer's expenses, the market price may fail to reflect the full social cost. Likewise, a buyer may be unaware of a defect, or a household may be unable to afford something it badly needs. An economic model of competitive markets helps explain how prices coordinate choices under specified assumptions, but those assumptions matter. When interpreting a price, distinguish the market signal from other evidence about safety, fairness and quality. A higher price does not by itself prove superior workmanship, and a lower price does not prove a product is undesirable. The useful habit is to ask what information the price communicates and what important facts still require investigation."},"127":{"body":"In 1734 a chart of the Philippine islands was published in Manila. The Library of Congress preserves a copy under its Spanish title, Carta hydrographica y chorographica de las Yslas Filipinas. The chart associated with Pedro Murillo Velarde offers a historical representation of the archipelago, not a modern satellite view. Its production required selection, measurement, drawing and engraving. The finished work combines geographic information with surrounding illustrations. Such details make it possible to investigate not only the places represented but also the purposes and conventions of eighteenth-century mapmaking.","extra":"When examining an old map, begin with its date, title and publication information. Then notice how coastlines, names, insets and decorative elements are arranged. Which places receive the greatest detail? What is presented as important to the intended viewer? Compare the geographic outline with a modern map, but avoid assuming that every discrepancy has the same explanation. Differences may reflect the available observations, surveying techniques or the needs of the people who commissioned the chart. Its illustrations are historical representations made in a colonial setting, rather than neutral records of every community. The map's value is therefore wider than its geographical accuracy. It provides a starting point for studying how knowledge of the islands was gathered, organized and circulated, and how a historical artifact can preserve evidence about the society that produced it."},"140":{"body":"Deep below the ocean's surface, seawater can enter cracks in the crust, absorb geothermal heat and dissolve substances from the surrounding rocks. It then rises and emerges through hydrothermal vents. NOAA reports that some vent fluids exceed 340 degrees Celsius. At ordinary atmospheric pressure, pure water boils near 100 degrees Celsius, but boiling depends on pressure as well as temperature. The enormous pressure exerted by the overlying ocean changes the conditions under which liquid water can persist. Hot water can therefore emerge from a vent at temperatures that would be impossible for an uncovered pot of liquid water at sea level.","extra":"When vent fluid encounters the much colder ocean, dissolved materials can form solid particles. These particles may create dark clouds above the vents and accumulate into chimney-like structures. The dark appearance is not smoke from an ordinary fire. The surrounding environment also supports unusual ecosystems: microorganisms can use chemical energy to make organic matter, providing a foundation for other organisms in the absence of direct sunlight. Not every vent has identical fluid temperatures, chemistry or inhabitants. The example brings together geology, physics, chemistry and biology, showing how a single environmental process can affect an entire local ecosystem. A useful comparison is a pressure cooker, which permits water to reach a higher temperature than an open pot. The enormous pressures of the deep ocean represent a much more extreme application of the underlying principle."}})){const p=CONTENT.find(x=>x.id===Number(id));Object.assign(p,revision,{editorialReview:{date:'2026-11-27',status:'source-aligned revision',scope:'Direct or indexed source checks; two LOC direct requests blocked. Comparative advantage source corrected. Original explanatory examples; not independent peer review.'}})}

