for(const [id,revision] of Object.entries({"66":{"body":"Imagine that you have an uncommitted hour on a Saturday. Three possibilities are available: read an interesting chapter, walk outside, or prepare a meal for later. If reading is your preferred choice and walking would be your next choice, the opportunity cost of reading is the benefit you would have received from walking. It is not the combined benefit of every activity you did not choose. Economists use opportunity cost to make scarcity visible: time, money and other resources cannot normally be devoted to every possible purpose at once. A choice can therefore have a real cost without involving any payment.","extra":"The idea becomes useful when comparing options with very different prices. A cheap household item that takes an afternoon to repair may cost more in time than you are willing to give up; alternatively, repairing it might be enjoyable and teach a skill. Your best alternative depends on what is feasible and valuable to you at that moment. Do not assign an imaginary hourly wage to every moment of rest. Rest, worship, conversation and play can themselves be the most valuable use of available time. The exercise is to recognize meaningful alternatives before committing, rather than discovering them only afterward. Before accepting an optional task, ask what you would otherwise do with that time and whether the new task is worth the exchange."},"67":{"body":"Suppose you bought a book for five hundred pesos and have already spent three evenings reading it. The remaining chapters appear unlikely to answer your question. If the purchase cannot be refunded, neither reading another chapter nor setting the book aside will recover the money. Similarly, those three evenings cannot be recovered by completing the book. Economists call expenditures that cannot be undone sunk costs. They differ from the future time or money that your decision can still affect. A useful question is whether the remaining reading is worth the attention it would require today, given what you have learned about the book so far.","extra":"This principle does not make quitting inherently wise. A demanding book may become rewarding after its introductory chapters, and a course may require you to finish a particular text. Completing a commitment can also serve a purpose beyond immediate enjoyment. Those are reasons to continue because of expected future benefits, not because of irreversible expenditure. Likewise, if a book can still be resold, its resale value is relevant to the choice; the entire purchase price should not automatically be treated as irrecoverable. Distinguish changing direction after receiving new evidence from abandoning an activity whenever it becomes difficult. You might set a review point, read one more chapter deliberately, and then decide whether to continue. A sound decision can follow an earlier mistake without needing to justify it."},"71":{"body":"The Pillars of Creation are columns of gas and dust within the Eagle Nebula. In familiar visible-light photographs, portions of the columns look opaque because intervening material scatters and absorbs light. The James Webb Space Telescope observes at infrared wavelengths, which can pass through some dusty regions more effectively. Its Near-Infrared Camera therefore reveals objects and structures that are less apparent in visible-light views. NASA identifies young stars and features associated with material expelled by developing stars in its image. The additional view does not make the earlier photograph obsolete: each observation records a different part of the physical situation.","extra":"A telescope does not simply reproduce the experience of looking through a window. Instruments measure radiation in selected wavelength ranges; astronomers can then assign visible colors to those measurements. The color key is important because it explains what each displayed color represents. A richly colored infrared picture need not resemble what a human eye would see if it could visit the nebula. Nor does infrared observation penetrate every cloud without obstruction. Different materials, wavelengths and conditions affect what becomes detectable. Comparing images can help investigators separate features associated with stars from features associated with intervening dust and gas. Try inspecting the two views before reading their captions: identify a structure common to both, then find a feature that becomes clearer in one. The contrast illustrates how scientific knowledge grows when instruments supply complementary evidence."},"72":{"body":"The Blue Marble photograph, made during Apollo 17's voyage to the Moon in 1972, shows the illuminated Earth against the darkness of space. Familiar continents appear alongside extensive oceans and swirling clouds. A political map is designed to communicate borders and names; a spacecraft photograph instead records the light reaching its camera from a particular viewpoint. Its lack of drawn boundaries is not a claim that political boundaries are unimportant. Rather, the image makes visible a different level of organization: the land, atmosphere and water that form a connected physical planet.","extra":"Consider a cloud system crossing a coastline. The movement is governed by atmospheric conditions rather than the distinction between land owned by different communities. Rain falling inland can feed rivers that eventually reach the ocean. Processes connect places that might appear separate on an ordinary map. Yet the photograph alone cannot establish where a storm will travel, measure the temperature of the sea or describe changes over decades. Researchers need repeated observations and measurements to investigate those questions. This is why an extraordinary single image and a long series of less dramatic observations can both be scientifically valuable. When using the Blue Marble as a historical or scientific source, distinguish the visible features from the broader conclusions the image suggests. The photograph invites a planetary perspective while reminding us that a viewpoint, however impressive, is still only one viewpoint."},"110":{"body":"In December 1968, Apollo 8 carried astronauts into orbit around the Moon. During the mission William Anders photographed Earth appearing beyond the lunar horizon, creating the image known as Earthrise. For people accustomed to seeing the Moon from Earth, the photograph offered an inversion of a familiar relationship. The foreground is another world's gray terrain, while the place from which nearly all earlier human observations had been made appears in the distance. The apparent rising of Earth in this setting resulted from the movement of the orbiting spacecraft relative to the lunar horizon.","extra":"Perspective matters here in both a literal and a figurative sense. An observer standing at a fixed location on much of the Moon's near side would not normally see Earth rise and set every lunar day in the manner that the Sun does on Earth. The Moon's rotation is synchronized with its orbit, so Earth stays in roughly the same direction for such an observer. The moving spacecraft changes the viewing geometry. The photograph thus records a real scene while also depending on the particular circumstances of its creation. During Apollo 8's Christmas Eve broadcast, the crew also read from Genesis, providing another example of how the mission became meaningful beyond its scientific objectives. An image may have physical, historical and cultural significance at the same time. Each kind of significance can be investigated without treating any one interpretation as the complete explanation of its importance."},"115":{"body":"Emanuel Leutze's Washington Crossing the Delaware was completed in 1851, decades after the American Revolutionary War episode it represents. Its scale and composition invite the viewer to focus on a determined central figure, a flag and a boat carrying people through difficult conditions. The arrangement is an artistic construction. Leutze chose how to organize the figures, distribute light and direct attention, rather than recording a moment with a camera. The Metropolitan Museum of Art describes the painting as a work that acquired major cultural significance in both Germany and the United States. Its later reception is part of its historical importance.","extra":"Historians can ask at least two different questions about the canvas. What can it tell us about the actual eighteenth-century event? What can it tell us about nineteenth-century ideas concerning revolution, political leadership and national identity? For the first question, the painting needs to be compared with records closer to the event. For the second, its composition, exhibition history and reception become evidence in their own right. This distinction applies to historical films, monuments and illustrations as well. A representation can express a serious interpretation of the past without being a literal reconstruction. Conversely, a visually compelling work does not establish the accuracy of every depicted detail. Inspect the choices the artist made, then ask which claims can be supported by separate evidence. This preserves both the artistic value of the work and the discipline of historical inquiry."},"117":{"body":"Vincent van Gogh's 1887 Self-Portrait with a Straw Hat belongs to a period in which he repeatedly painted himself while living in Paris. The Metropolitan Museum of Art connects this practice to his limited resources and his desire to improve as a figure painter. A mirror gave him a subject he could study repeatedly without arranging or paying for another person's time. The canvas also bears an earlier study on its other side. These circumstances help explain why self-portraiture was not simply an exercise in recording one's appearance: it was also a practical means of sustained artistic experimentation.","extra":"Observe how individual strokes establish the face, background and hat. Rather than blending every boundary into a smooth transition, the artist uses marks and color relationships that remain visible. The result provides a way to study how different colors influence their neighbors and how a collection of marks can become a recognizable form at a distance. A learner can adopt the underlying method without imitating the finished painting: select an available subject, repeat the exercise under different conditions and compare results. Repetition becomes useful when it tests a particular choice rather than mechanically reproducing a previous attempt. The artist's personal circumstances provide context, but facial expression and brushwork alone cannot establish a medical diagnosis or a specific private emotion. The evidence supports a more grounded discussion of materials, technique, artistic constraints and deliberate practice."},"119":{"body":"On March 11, 2024, the Mars Odyssey spacecraft obtained an unusual view of Olympus Mons. Instead of observing primarily toward the surface beneath it, the spacecraft was oriented so that its Thermal Emission Imaging System could observe toward the Martian horizon. The resulting image includes the enormous volcanic landform and bands within the atmosphere. NASA explains that the observations help researchers investigate clouds and dust at different heights. The scene shows why an instrument's scientific usefulness depends partly on the direction in which it is pointed, not simply on the quality of its detector.","extra":"A downward view is valuable for mapping the extent and arrangement of surface features. A horizon view can provide a different geometry for investigating the atmosphere: material distributed at different elevations can contribute to the appearance of distinct layers. However, color differences should not automatically be interpreted as proof of a particular substance. The interpretation depends on the instrument and other information about atmospheric conditions. Repeating similar observations in different Martian seasons gives investigators a basis for comparing changes over time. Consider how photographing the sky above a mountain from the ground differs from photographing the mountain from directly overhead. Each view answers some questions better than others. The lesson is broader than Mars: when an existing measurement does not reveal the feature of interest, changing the observational approach may be more useful than simply collecting more measurements in the same way."},"141":{"body":"A telescope normally gathers light using mirrors or lenses. In astronomy another kind of lens can arise from the presence of a massive object between the observer and a more distant source. According to general relativity, mass changes the geometry of spacetime, and light follows paths through that geometry. A foreground galaxy or cluster can therefore redirect light that would otherwise travel along different paths. Depending on the arrangement, the background source may appear enlarged, brighter, distorted into arcs or represented by several images. NASA's gravitational-lensing diagram illustrates how light traveling toward Earth can be redirected by an intervening cluster.","extra":"The image received by a telescope is not necessarily a straightforward picture of the original background object. Scientists model the intervening mass and compare the predictions of that model with the observed patterns. The distortions can supply evidence about the foreground distribution of matter, including matter that is difficult to detect through its own emitted light. Magnification can also make a distant source easier to study. The two objectives are related but distinct: understanding the lens and understanding the object whose light passes near it. Neither is achieved merely by recognizing an arc in a photograph. Measurement, geometry and uncertainty all enter the interpretation. A useful intellectual lesson is that a distortion can contain information. If the mechanism producing it is understood well enough, what first seems like an obstacle to observation can become a method of investigation."},"143":{"body":"José Rizal and Andrés Bonifacio contributed to the development of Filipino nationalism under Spanish rule, but their historical roles were not interchangeable. Rizal's novels exposed injustices and encouraged discussion of the conditions of colonial society. In 1892 he established La Liga Filipina, an association concerned with collective improvement and reform. Bonifacio joined the association and subsequently helped establish the Katipunan in the same year. The Library of Congress research guide describes the emergence of the latter organization after Rizal's arrest, as Bonifacio pursued independence through revolution. These developments show a movement containing different organizations, strategies and changing circumstances rather than a single unified program.","extra":"It is tempting to make national heroes into symbols of opposite temperaments, as though their differences could be summarized by saying one wrote while the other acted. That contrast obscures their organizational work, the changing political environment and the varied aims of the people involved. A better historical approach compares the dates of their activities, the purposes of the associations they joined or founded, and evidence of their documented positions. It also avoids using either figure as a substitute for the many other Filipinos who contributed to the movement. Their importance can be acknowledged without assuming that they agreed on every question or that one person's strategy provides a complete explanation of the revolution. Studying them together helps reveal how ideas, institutions and political circumstances interacted during the struggle for national self-determination."}})){const p=CONTENT.find(x=>x.id===Number(id));Object.assign(p,revision,{editorialReview:{date:'2026-11-27',status:'source-aligned revision',scope:'Original reference retrieved directly; independent explanatory examples, not independent peer review.'}})}

